--TEST--
A `self` return is the declaring class however the call was reached, while a `static` return is the class the call named — `rule:statements/static-is-a-member-modifier`, `rule:types/grammar`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:types/void-never-self-static
// The two class-relative return types differ in which class they name. `self`
// is the class the method was written in, so what comes back is typed as that
// class wherever it was reached from; `static` is the class the call itself
// named, so a base class hands back whichever subclass was asked for. Neither
// touches dispatch: the value keeps the class it really is either way.
class Base {
    public static function make(): static {
        return new static();
    }

    public function same(): self {
        return $this;
    }

    public function name(): string {
        return "base";
    }
}

class Child extends Base {
    public function name(): string {
        return "child";
    }
}

// `static` is the called class, so these two calls answer two classes.
echo Base::make()->name(), " ", Child::make()->name(), "\n";
echo Child::make() is Child ? "child" : "not child", "\n";

// `self` is `Base` however it was reached, and what comes back still
// dispatches on the class it really is.
Child $c = new Child();
Base $b = $c->same();
echo $b->name(), " ", $c->same()->name(), "\n";
echo $b is Child ? "still a child" : "no longer a child", "\n";
--EXPECT--
base child
child
child child
still a child
